From 628270138af03fd452559d959631eb1857cf1649 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 15:19:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dvbridge: a picture takes the RPU of its enhancement picture

In a dual-layer Dolby Vision stream the RPU travels in the access unit
of an enhancement picture and is the metadata of that picture. A stream
of one track carries the access units of the two layers in pairs, in
coding order, and FFmpeg's decoder attaches the RPU of a packet to the
base picture of that packet. When the two layers are coded in the same
picture order that is the right picture. When they are not, the pair of a
packet is two different pictures, and the RPU went to the wrong one.

Measured on the test stream of two tracks (2876 access units with an
RPU; the enhancement picture of an access unit is presented one picture
before the base picture in 1881 of them and two after it in 940): with
the RPU on the picture its enhancement access unit presents, the level 1
metadata takes 15 values, each in one run of pictures; on the base
picture of the same packet it comes in 20 runs, back and forth around
each change. The enhancement pictures themselves were already paired by
picture order.

Now:
- the enhancement layer is split off with its RPU (dovi_split mode
  el_rpu), so its decoder attaches the RPU to the enhancement picture;
- when a base picture is paired with its enhancement picture, and that
  one brings an RPU of its own that is not the RPU the base picture came
  with, the base picture takes it. The same RPU changes nothing: a stream
  whose layers share their picture order plays as before, to the byte.
  The rule is one function of the module, dvbridge_fel_layer_metadata(),
  so that the host test runs what Kodi runs;
- a base picture that goes out without its enhancement picture (the
  layer resynchronizes, software decoding) keeps the RPU it came with,
  as before. In a stream of this kind that is the RPU of a neighbouring
  picture, and those pictures are counted;
- the coded RPU of a base picture is only kept while an enhancement
  layer is decoded, which is the only reader of it;
- the log says once that the stream is of this kind, and at the end how
  many pictures took another RPU and how many went out without their
  enhancement picture. It also says once when the base pictures bring
  RPUs and the enhancement pictures none: FFmpeg's decoder does not take
  the first unit of a packet as an RPU, and an RPU that comes before the
  enhancement units is the first unit of what the layer's decoder gets.
  Such a stream plays as before this change.

Tested on the host with the module's own code and the test stream
decoded in software, the module's decoder on one thread as in Kodi
(tests/fel_rpu_test.c): with lengths and with start codes (the framing
of a stream that comes in two tracks), with a packet that lost its
enhancement units, and with the base layer on one thread too. 2523 of
2876 paired pictures take another RPU; the two pictures the enhancement
layer does not have keep theirs, which adds two runs.

The test does not tie an RPU to the content of its picture: a constant
shift between the two would pass, and the stream has no cut to tell.
That the RPU of an enhancement access unit belongs to the picture that
access unit presents is how the two-track form of the stream times
them, and how the Intel fork's Blu-ray reader matches them. Not verified
on hardware. Whether discs code their layers in different picture order
is not known; the log lines will tell.
